# Not written:
#   SSH for the partner - pass: the rule is for zone WAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Ping from the inside: the rule is for zone LAN, DMZ, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   DNS from the firewall: the rule is for zone LAN, GUEST, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   NAT Web server HTTPS: the rule is for zone WAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   NAT Application ports: the rule is for zone WAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Mail in: the rule is for zone WAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Web server over IPv6: the rule is for zone WAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Ping and errors to the firewall: the rule is for zone WAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Administrators to the servers: the rule is for zone LAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Users to the web servers: the rule is for zone LAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Users to the web server by its outside address: the rule is for zone LAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Default allow LAN to any rule: the rule is for zone LAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Default allow LAN IPv6 to any rule: the rule is for zone LAN, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Servers fetch their updates: host name object mirror.example.org, updates.example.com: a security rule names addresses; the rule is for zone DMZ, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Mail out, not to the inside: not converted: negated destination that does not name the same on both sides of NAT rule 'Web server HTTPS' (198.51.100.10, 192.168.20.10); not converted: negated destination that does not name the same on both sides of NAT rule 'Application ports' (198.51.100.10, 192.168.20.11); the rule is for zone DMZ, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Syslog to the collector: schedule 'OfficeHours' is not converted; the rule would apply at all times; the rule is for zone DMZ, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Nothing from the list: references 'Blocklist': a URL table: its addresses are in a file on the firewall, not in the configuration
#   Tunnel of the old application: disabled in the source
#   Guests browse: the rule is for zone GUEST, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
#   Phones of the guests: not converted: it matches a mark set by another rule (tagged) 'VOICE'; the rule is for zone GUEST, and a security rule names no zone: give the networks behind it with --zone-network
